1
00:00:00,697 --> 00:00:05,002
I ate my first mango in a hole.

2
00:00:05,002 --> 00:00:06,837
What some called a shoe,

3
00:00:06,837 --> 00:00:10,240
the box, Ad-Seg, lock up.

4
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:13,610
But no matter what you call solitary confinement,

5
00:00:13,610 --> 00:00:17,614
no one expects it to be where you eat your first mango.

6
00:00:17,614 --> 00:00:19,616
(Laughter)

7
00:00:19,616 --> 00:00:21,285
And me?

8
00:00:21,285 --> 00:00:22,920
I thought the oval-shaped fruit

9
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:27,391
that came with lunch that day was an apple.

10
00:00:27,391 --> 00:00:32,496
I've been teaching myself to juggle, to keep from losing my mind.

11
00:00:32,496 --> 00:00:36,266
Imagining that I could manage my troubles

12
00:00:36,266 --> 00:00:40,537
if I could float apples into an orbit of my creation.

13
00:00:40,537 --> 00:00:43,440
When I sank my teeth into the unfamiliar,

14
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:45,976
fragrant fruit,

15
00:00:45,976 --> 00:00:49,546
juices ran down my fingertips.

16
00:00:49,546 --> 00:00:52,649
I ate until I chipped a tooth,

17
00:00:52,649 --> 00:00:57,788
scraping it along the mango seed.

18
00:00:57,788 --> 00:01:01,825
I never saw another mango in prison.

19
00:01:01,825 --> 00:01:04,861
And I only learned the name of the fruit

20
00:01:04,861 --> 00:01:08,365
when I was home and in my mother's kitchen.

21
00:01:08,365 --> 00:01:09,900
Before I started juggling,

22
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:14,571
I trashed the mostly rotten apples without a second glance.

23
00:01:14,571 --> 00:01:20,644
Then juggling transformed the apples into my way of escape.

24
00:01:20,644 --> 00:01:26,216
That's how prison taught me that you find what you look for.

25
00:01:26,216 --> 00:01:32,189
Who would expect to hear a story about prison that includes a mango?

26
00:01:32,189 --> 00:01:35,325
I got locked up in 1996.

27
00:01:35,325 --> 00:01:36,827
I was 16 years old,

28
00:01:36,827 --> 00:01:40,797
five-foot-five and 125 pounds.

29
00:01:40,797 --> 00:01:43,133
I hadn't won a fight since the second grade.

30
00:01:43,133 --> 00:01:44,668
(Laughter)

31
00:01:44,668 --> 00:01:49,840
Becoming a brawler overnight wasn't in the cards for me.

32
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:54,244
That's why people often ask me how I survived.

33
00:01:54,244 --> 00:01:56,179
What if I say,

34
00:01:56,179 --> 00:02:00,951
I survived prison because I believed you find what you look for.

35
00:02:00,951 --> 00:02:04,454
Because of what it means to notice the mango.

36
00:02:04,454 --> 00:02:08,158
To commit yourself to the possibility of a miracle,

37
00:02:08,158 --> 00:02:11,795
even without a name for the wonder.

38
00:02:11,795 --> 00:02:14,231
I should thank my judge.

39
00:02:14,231 --> 00:02:17,401
After I pleaded guilty to carjacking,

40
00:02:17,401 --> 00:02:20,303
and he sentenced me to nine years in prison,

41
00:02:20,303 --> 00:02:26,309
I was certain that I was going home.

42
00:02:26,309 --> 00:02:30,781
I waited for him to tell me it was all a dream.

43
00:02:30,781 --> 00:02:33,984
Then he confirmed my intuition.

44
00:02:33,984 --> 00:02:38,288
“I don’t have any illusion[s] that sending you to the penitentiary

45
00:02:38,288 --> 00:02:41,291
is going to help."

46
00:02:41,291 --> 00:02:45,929
Do you know what the cruelest word in the English language is?

47
00:02:45,929 --> 00:02:48,732
The preposition "but."

48
00:02:48,732 --> 00:02:53,804
The judge said, "But you can go down there

49
00:02:53,804 --> 00:02:59,142
and you can make the best of it or not."

50
00:02:59,142 --> 00:03:04,081
I figured the judge was saying

51
00:03:04,081 --> 00:03:07,551
that I could choose what I looked for in those cells.

52
00:03:07,551 --> 00:03:11,688
Becoming a computer engineer was now out of the question.

53
00:03:11,688 --> 00:03:17,494
Earning a college degree from prison was just as unlikely.

54
00:03:17,494 --> 00:03:20,063
But because of what the judge said,

55
00:03:20,063 --> 00:03:24,601
I thought not only of what I would lose.

56
00:03:24,601 --> 00:03:29,773
I was 16 years old, planning who I would be when I left.

57
00:03:29,773 --> 00:03:34,044
I've loved books since I was a kid.

58
00:03:34,044 --> 00:03:36,713
And so on my sentencing day,

59
00:03:36,713 --> 00:03:40,417
I told myself that I would be a writer.

60
00:03:40,417 --> 00:03:42,152
In a crucible that is prison,

61
00:03:42,152 --> 00:03:45,388
I learned to see other people.

62
00:03:45,388 --> 00:03:47,791
I remember Star.

63
00:03:47,791 --> 00:03:51,161
We spent months in adjacent cells in a hole.

64
00:03:51,161 --> 00:03:55,699
We were both obsessed with stories of our past

65
00:03:55,699 --> 00:03:58,902
and with fantasy novels like "Lord of the Rings,"

66
00:03:58,902 --> 00:04:02,372
books that let us imagine ourselves

67
00:04:02,372 --> 00:04:07,677
as people who chose to be heroes instead of villains.

68
00:04:07,677 --> 00:04:09,746
He was a boxer.

69
00:04:09,746 --> 00:04:11,214
I wasn't.

70
00:04:11,214 --> 00:04:16,219
Star taught me what he knew, how to train, how to breathe,

71
00:04:16,219 --> 00:04:19,256
how not to let frustration and a lack of discipline

72
00:04:19,256 --> 00:04:22,392
turn into something that ends up costing you

73
00:04:22,392 --> 00:04:25,562
far more than you can afford to lose.

74
00:04:25,562 --> 00:04:27,964
He was not the only one.

75
00:04:27,964 --> 00:04:29,866
I remember Mike G.

76
00:04:29,866 --> 00:04:34,337
One day he asked, "If you slipped at McDonald's

77
00:04:34,337 --> 00:04:36,573
and broke your leg,

78
00:04:36,573 --> 00:04:40,010
and they had one of those 'wet floor' signs up,

79
00:04:40,010 --> 00:04:42,412
could you sue?"

80
00:04:42,412 --> 00:04:44,681
Of course I could sue.

81
00:04:44,681 --> 00:04:46,583
We argued.

82
00:04:46,583 --> 00:04:48,618
I knew I was right.

83
00:04:48,618 --> 00:04:52,389
An hour later he says, "You know you're wrong."

84
00:04:52,389 --> 00:04:55,325
And then he shows me a page

85
00:04:55,325 --> 00:05:00,764
in a faded yellow book with the word "Torts" on the cover.

86
00:05:00,764 --> 00:05:04,000
Who asks a question they know the answer to,

87
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,204
and then argues about it for an hour?

88
00:05:07,204 --> 00:05:09,606
(Laughter)

89
00:05:09,606 --> 00:05:13,577
The devil on my right shoulder said "Swing."

90
00:05:13,577 --> 00:05:17,781
Now remember, I hadn’t won a fight since the second grade.

91
00:05:17,781 --> 00:05:22,185
So instead, I picked up a tattered issue of prison legal news from his locker

92
00:05:22,185 --> 00:05:25,655
[and] an ad for a paralegal course caught my eye.

93
00:05:25,655 --> 00:05:28,825
My mom paid for the classes.

94
00:05:28,825 --> 00:05:31,127
I didn't want to be a lawyer.

95
00:05:31,127 --> 00:05:36,433
I just didn't want to be anybody's fool.

96
00:05:36,433 --> 00:05:39,236
I almost didn't finish the class.

97
00:05:39,236 --> 00:05:43,907
You see, I was at Sussex I State Prison,

98
00:05:43,907 --> 00:05:45,275
and they had these lines,

99
00:05:45,275 --> 00:05:49,079
these yellow lines that you cannot cross.

100
00:05:49,079 --> 00:05:51,915
But I was a perpetual line crosser.

101
00:05:51,915 --> 00:05:54,584
I would cross lines to get stamps,

102
00:05:54,584 --> 00:05:56,920
to say hello to friends,

103
00:05:56,920 --> 00:06:00,190
to borrow food, to give people food.

104
00:06:00,190 --> 00:06:04,661
One day, I crossed the yellow line to get a book.

105
00:06:04,661 --> 00:06:08,965
A new book is a rare occurrence in prison.

106
00:06:08,965 --> 00:06:13,403
"Betts, I'm so tired of you stepping over my lines.

107
00:06:13,403 --> 00:06:16,907
This time you're going back to the hole,"

108
00:06:16,907 --> 00:06:20,510
the lieutenant said before he cuffed me.

109
00:06:20,510 --> 00:06:23,780
From the second tier, Luke saw it all.

110
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:27,684
We hadn't exchanged more than a dozen words in his life,

111
00:06:27,684 --> 00:06:29,519
but when they opened his cell door,

112
00:06:29,519 --> 00:06:32,455
he pushed all his property out of his cell and said,

113
00:06:32,455 --> 00:06:36,593
"If Shah go to the hole, I go to the hole."

114
00:06:36,593 --> 00:06:38,995
Others began banging on their cell doors,

115
00:06:38,995 --> 00:06:44,868
also demanding to go to solitary confinement.

116
00:06:44,868 --> 00:06:47,137
It was another miracle.

117
00:06:47,137 --> 00:06:50,440
I didn't go to the hole that day.

118
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:53,143
Luke only knew me from the times we hooped together,

119
00:06:53,143 --> 00:06:57,747
but that was enough for him to see me as worthy of more than a cell.

120
00:06:57,747 --> 00:07:03,053
Even then, I guess I was finding what I was looking for.

121
00:07:03,053 --> 00:07:07,824
Now I left prison on March 4, 2005.

122
00:07:07,824 --> 00:07:09,025
Before April 1,

123
00:07:09,025 --> 00:07:12,796
I tried to enroll at the University of Maryland.

124
00:07:12,796 --> 00:07:16,466
I didn't even know what the word semester meant.

125
00:07:16,466 --> 00:07:21,171
When I told this kind counselor that I had just come home from prison,

126
00:07:21,171 --> 00:07:23,873
he suggested community college.

127
00:07:23,873 --> 00:07:25,976
I took refuge there.

128
00:07:25,976 --> 00:07:29,346
Then I started picking up college degrees like felonies.

129
00:07:29,346 --> 00:07:33,450
(Laughter)

130
00:07:33,450 --> 00:07:38,254
I had an AA, a BA, an MFA.

131
00:07:38,254 --> 00:07:41,257
I published a memoir and a collection of poetry

132
00:07:41,257 --> 00:07:45,061
before I turned 30 years old.

133
00:07:45,061 --> 00:07:50,100
(Applause)

134
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:53,570
But I couldn't even get an interview for a teaching gig.

135
00:07:53,570 --> 00:07:56,072
I was 33.

136
00:07:56,072 --> 00:07:58,608
I had two small sons [and] a felony record.

137
00:07:58,608 --> 00:08:00,610
I was unemployed.

138
00:08:00,610 --> 00:08:04,247
But I was once a jailhouse lawyer.

139
00:08:04,247 --> 00:08:07,650
And for a pack of tuna, a box of crackers and ramen noodles,

140
00:08:07,650 --> 00:08:11,521
I got five years off somebody's prison sentence.

141
00:08:11,521 --> 00:08:14,624
(Applause)

142
00:08:14,624 --> 00:08:17,494
I decided to go to law school.

143
00:08:17,494 --> 00:08:20,830
A year later, I [had] been admitted to as many law schools

144
00:08:20,830 --> 00:08:23,133
as prisons where I served time:

145
00:08:23,133 --> 00:08:26,469
Harvard, Yale, the University of Pennsylvania,

146
00:08:26,469 --> 00:08:29,639
Georgetown, Michigan.

147
00:08:29,639 --> 00:08:32,475
I went to Yale wanting to be a public defender,

148
00:08:32,475 --> 00:08:36,312
but I was working in a public defender's office,

149
00:08:36,312 --> 00:08:40,750
and I couldn't stomach my young clients being locked up.

150
00:08:40,750 --> 00:08:44,320
Every one of them reminded me of friends in prison doing football numbers:

151
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:48,425
30, 40, 50 years, life.

152
00:08:48,425 --> 00:08:51,828
Then Juvie asked me to help him get out.

153
00:08:51,828 --> 00:08:54,564
I started representing my friends,

154
00:08:54,564 --> 00:08:59,502
failing and failing and failing again.

155
00:08:59,502 --> 00:09:01,504
Then Luke found me.

156
00:09:01,504 --> 00:09:04,374
I hadn't talked to him in 20 years.

157
00:09:04,374 --> 00:09:08,244
I walked into the parole board with his blind mother on my arm

158
00:09:08,244 --> 00:09:11,848
as if I were her son.

159
00:09:11,848 --> 00:09:13,783
He made parole.

160
00:09:13,783 --> 00:09:17,554
Then Keith did, and then Fettes, then Juvie, and then Tyrell,

161
00:09:17,554 --> 00:09:19,789
and I got Dempsey out, too.

162
00:09:19,789 --> 00:09:24,894
Six men, all sent to prison as teenagers.

163
00:09:24,894 --> 00:09:27,197
All out

164
00:09:27,197 --> 00:09:31,301
because my mom spent money for me to take a paralegal course

165
00:09:31,301 --> 00:09:33,203
in the middle of a prison sentence

166
00:09:33,203 --> 00:09:37,240
that neither one of us knew if I would survive.

167
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:39,309
I left prison on March 4,

168
00:09:39,309 --> 00:09:42,745
the only date that is also a command,

169
00:09:42,745 --> 00:09:44,981
advising me to march forth out in the world

170
00:09:44,981 --> 00:09:48,485
and leave everything about prison behind.

171
00:09:48,485 --> 00:09:54,124
(Applause)

172
00:09:54,124 --> 00:09:58,495
But I found myself returning again and again.

173
00:09:58,495 --> 00:10:02,632
For friends, for clients, to read poems.

174
00:10:02,632 --> 00:10:04,467
So when someone asked,

175
00:10:04,467 --> 00:10:10,039
"What would you do for people in prison if money wasn't an issue?"

176
00:10:10,039 --> 00:10:14,210
I said, “We put millions of people in prison.

177
00:10:14,210 --> 00:10:18,481
I will put millions of books inside.”

178
00:10:18,481 --> 00:10:20,383
When she asked me how,

179
00:10:20,383 --> 00:10:23,786
I said, "One library at a time."

180
00:10:23,786 --> 00:10:27,123
In 2020, it was all a dream.

181
00:10:27,123 --> 00:10:28,925
A little over a year later,

182
00:10:28,925 --> 00:10:34,564
we opened our first Freedom Library in a gutted cell in MCI, Norfolk.

183
00:10:34,564 --> 00:10:37,400
A prison where Malcolm X served time.

184
00:10:37,400 --> 00:10:41,070
Our second was in Louisiana's Angola State Prison.

185
00:10:41,070 --> 00:10:45,575
We put freedom libraries on a cell block so that men can step away --

186
00:10:45,575 --> 00:10:50,413
No, so that men, women and children can step away from their bunks

187
00:10:50,413 --> 00:10:52,515
and reach for a book

188
00:10:52,515 --> 00:10:55,952
that will be the start of the freedom they look for.

189
00:10:55,952 --> 00:10:58,321
We've opened more than 600 Freedom Libraries,

190
00:10:58,321 --> 00:11:01,057
putting books I once dreamed of reading

191
00:11:01,057 --> 00:11:03,626
into the hands of others.

192
00:11:03,626 --> 00:11:06,796
What we've heard often makes me weep.

193
00:11:06,796 --> 00:11:07,997
Like when Hillary,

194
00:11:07,997 --> 00:11:12,802
one of the first women in prison to touch a Freedom Library, wrote,

195
00:11:12,802 --> 00:11:15,104
"The smell of wood and the smell of a new book

196
00:11:15,104 --> 00:11:17,874
takes me right out of this place.

197
00:11:17,874 --> 00:11:22,478
I have been incarcerated for 20 years, and I’m doing life.

198
00:11:22,478 --> 00:11:24,948
I love books and I'm an avid reader.

199
00:11:24,948 --> 00:11:27,617
I have been since I was a little girl."

200
00:11:27,617 --> 00:11:30,954
Hillary makes me think about myself.

201
00:11:30,954 --> 00:11:36,292
30 years ago, I walked into a prison barely older than my 14-year-old son.

202
00:11:36,292 --> 00:11:39,462
My 18-year-old is choosing a college.

203
00:11:39,462 --> 00:11:41,731
I am the father I am

204
00:11:41,731 --> 00:11:45,168
because of what I found in those prison cells.

205
00:11:45,168 --> 00:11:48,738
Long ago, I found out that you [find] what you look for

206
00:11:48,738 --> 00:11:53,876
because there is always someone who believes that you'll be searching.

207
00:11:53,876 --> 00:11:57,213
These days, I've returned to prisons with something as improbable

208
00:11:57,213 --> 00:12:00,717
as the mango I once found in solitary confinement.

209
00:12:00,717 --> 00:12:04,887
Luke and Tyrell have both returned to open Freedom Libraries with me.

210
00:12:04,887 --> 00:12:07,957
The men who taught me how to hold on to hope

211
00:12:07,957 --> 00:12:11,261
are now handing it out to others.

212
00:12:11,261 --> 00:12:14,063
Unnamed, unannounced,

213
00:12:14,063 --> 00:12:15,898
one book at a time.

214
00:12:15,898 --> 00:12:17,100
Thank you.

215
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:21,100
(Cheers and applause)
